> We could make setAttribute("nonce", "whatever") set the internal slot. There is not a named hook here, but lots of things act when attributes change (style attr parsing, "update the iframe attributes" steps, etc, etc). We could use a similar mechanism here. If we do that, then we'll just need to set the attr value to "" on insertion into the DOM and not actually copy it over to the internal slot, right?

I like this idea.

> I'm not sure I quite understand the proposal. In the testcase there are three "assignments": setting the content attribute, setting the idl attribute, whatever work insertion into the DOM does. What exactly are you proposing those three things do?

I think I'm now suggesting:

1.  Setting the content attribute updates the internal slot with the attribute's value. (Something like "Whenever a `NoncedElement`'s `nonce` content attribute is set or changed, [process its `nonce` attribute].")

2. Setting the idl attribute updates the internal slot with the new value.
3. Insertion clears the content attribute, iff a header-delivered CSP is set.

That is:

```
var x = document.createElement('script');
assert_equals(x.nonce, '');
assert_equals(x.getAttribute('nonce'), null);

x.setAttribute('nonce', 'whatever');
assert_equals(x.nonce, 'whatever');
assert_equals(x.getAttribute('nonce'), 'whatever');

x.nonce = 'something';
assert_equals(x.nonce, 'something');
assert_equals(x.getAttribute('nonce'), 'whatever');

document.body.appendChild(x);
assert_equals(x.nonce, 'something');
assert_equals(x.getAttribute('nonce'), '');
```

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
...<script>
customElements.define("nonce-nonce", class Nonce extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
  }
  attributeChangedCallback(name, oldValue, newValue) {
    w(name + ": " + newValue);
  }
  connectedCallback() {
    w(this.getAttribute("nonce"));
  }
});
</script>
<nonce-nonce nonce=nonce>
```
The only thing I do not understand is why Chrome does not run my `attributeChangedCallback`. When that runs I would expect `nonce` to still have a value, but it would be good for @domenic to confirm, maybe I'm wrong. (When the constructor runs there ought to be no attributes.)

> Did this ever get second-implementer interest? Talk of commit messages feels a bit premature.

@arturjanc raised it again at TPAC, and my impression is that folks around the room nodded along. There were certainly no objections from anyone, but no roar of explicit support.

@ckerschb filed the Mozilla bug a while ago, perhaps he has opinions (or @dveditz?). @patrickkettner might be able to provide feedback from Edge? @johnwilander or @dbates-wk could weigh in from WebKit?
